{
  "dom_id": "step-q57157",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "$1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\times 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}=3\\,\\text{R}3=3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\n$$",
